2️⃣ Logical Layers → Tool Mapping
Below is the core table new joinees must memorize.
Logical Layer	Purpose	Tool Used	Why This Tool
Source of Truth	Track all changes	Git	Versioning, audit, collaboration
Validation & Automation	Orchestrate flow	Jenkins	Automates steps reliably
Artifact Creation	Package application	Docker	Consistent runtime everywhere
Infrastructure Intent	Define environments	Terraform	Repeatable infra creation
Configuration	Configure systems	Ansible	Idempotent configuration
Platform Runtime	Run applications	Kubernetes / OpenShift	Scaling, self-healing
OS Foundation	Base system	Linux	Stable, standard runtime
Observability	See system health	OpenShift/K8s native + tools	Metrics, logs, alerts
________________________________________
3️⃣ End-to-End Flow (Mapped Step-by-Step)
🔁 One Change Journey (New Joinee Friendly)
________________________________________
STEP 1️⃣ Source of Truth → Git
What happens:
•	Developer commits application code
•	Configurations and infra definitions are also versioned
Why Git here:
•	Single starting point
•	Full history
•	Rollback possible
🧠 Training takeaway:
“If it’s not in Git, it doesn’t exist.”
________________________________________
STEP 2️⃣ Automation Orchestration → Jenkins
What Jenkins does (logically):
•	Detects a change
•	Executes steps in a defined order
•	Stops the process if something fails
Jenkins responsibility:
•	Flow control
•	Visibility
•	Repeatability
🧠 Training takeaway:
“Jenkins doesn’t deploy — it coordinates.”
________________________________________
STEP 3️⃣ Artifact Creation → Docker
What happens conceptually:
•	Application + dependencies are packaged
•	Output is immutable (cannot be changed)
Why Docker here:
•	Same behavior everywhere
•	No “works on my machine” problem
🧠 Training takeaway:
“Docker freezes the application in time.”
________________________________________
STEP 4️⃣ Infrastructure Definition → Terraform
What Terraform handles:
•	Servers
•	Clusters
•	Networking
•	Storage (conceptually)
Why Terraform:
•	Infrastructure becomes code
•	Environments can be recreated anytime
🧠 Training takeaway:
“Terraform defines what infrastructure should exist.”
________________________________________
STEP 5️⃣ System Configuration → Ansible
What Ansible handles:
•	OS-level setup
•	Middleware configuration
•	System consistency
Why Ansible here:
•	Idempotent (safe to repeat)
•	Human-readable
•	Agentless
🧠 Training takeaway:
“Ansible defines how systems are configured.”
________________________________________
STEP 6️⃣ Platform Runtime → Kubernetes / OpenShift
What happens here:
•	Application runs inside a managed platform
•	Platform handles:
o	Scheduling
o	Restarting failures
o	Scaling
Why OpenShift over plain Kubernetes (enterprise view):
•	Security defaults
•	Integrated tooling
•	Enterprise governance
🧠 Training takeaway:
“OpenShift runs the app so humans don’t have to babysit it.”
________________________________________
STEP 7️⃣ Operating System Foundation → Linux
Linux role:
•	Base OS for:
o	Jenkins
o	Nodes
o	Build agents
Why Linux:
•	Stable
•	Secure
•	Industry standard
🧠 Training takeaway:
“Everything eventually runs on Linux.”
________________________________________
STEP 8️⃣ Observability & Feedback → OpenShift + Kubernetes Ecosystem
What is observed:
•	Is the app running?
•	Is it healthy?
•	Is it slow?
•	Is it failing?
Why observability matters:
•	Decisions are data-driven
•	Faster incident response
🧠 Training takeaway:
“No visibility = no control.”
________________________________________
4️⃣ Failure Handling (Mapped)
Failure Scenario	Tool Handling It
Build fails	Jenkins stops flow
Bad package	Docker version rollback
Infra issue	Terraform re-apply
Config issue	Ansible correction
App crash	OpenShift restarts
Performance drop	Observability alerts
🧠 Key lesson:
“Failures are isolated, not catastrophic.”
________________________________________
5️⃣ Security Mapping (High Level)
Security Area	Tool
Access control	OpenShift RBAC
Secrets	Platform-managed secrets
Audit	Git + Jenkins logs
OS hardening	Ansible
Platform isolation	Kubernetes/OpenShift
🧠 Training takeaway:
“Security is layered, not a single tool.”
________________________________________
